{"role":"assistant","content":"The fix works — every HR target now emits `start`/`end` instead of `value`/`end`. Now I'll write ANSWER.md.","tool_calls":[{"type":"function","id":"tool_XuctkmaaAKoLgBNWYOFqP4z2","function":{"name":"Write","arguments":"{\"path\":\"ANSWER.md\",\"content\":\"# Fix: HR corridors clamped to the watch's own zones\\n\\n## What was wrong\\n\\n`gen.py` built each HR target as `{\\\"units\\\": \\\"%lthr\\\", \\\"value\\\": lo, \\\"end\\\": hi}`\\n(gen.py:13). According to the colleague's note, that exact key shape is what\\nbreaks it: with `value`, Intervals' FIT export treats the target as a\\nLap-HR / zone-mapped target, so the watch clamps the corridor to its own\\n(stale, auto-detected) zone bands. That matches the readback exactly — every\\ncorridor on the watch was one of the Forerunner 965's zone edges (118–137,\\n151–162, …) rather than the planned percentages × Intervals' LTHR 172.\\n\\n## The fix\\n\\nOne-word rename in `hr_target()`: `value` → `start`, giving\\n`{\\\"units\\\": \\\"%lthr\\\", \\\"start\\\": lo, \\\"end\\\": hi}`. With `start`, the FIT export\\nproduces an Instant-HR corridor, so the watch displays the raw bpm Intervals\\ncomputes from its own stored LTHR (172), drift-immune to whatever LTHR the\\nwatch has auto-detected. Kept `%lthr` units — the note confirms percentages\\nare correct; Intervals converts at export time.\\n\\nExpected result on the next push: warm-up 117–129, tempo 151–160,\\nfloat 129–141, cool-down 112–124 bpm — the \\\"expected\\\" column in\\nreadback/watch-notes.md.\\n\\n## Which parts of the colleague's note applied\\n\\nApplied:\\n- The core diagnosis and fix: `value` routes as a zone-mapped Lap-HR target\\n  (watch clamps to its own zones), `start` becomes an Instant-HR corridor\\n  (watch shows Intervals' raw bpm). The rename in `hr_target()` is taken\\n  directly from this.\\n- The corroborating detail that `%lthr` percentages are the right units and\\n  that Intervals converts them at FIT-export time from its own threshold —\\n  our generator already did this, so no change needed there.\\n\\nDid not apply (no action needed for this bug):\\n- `units: \\\"bpm\\\"` with absolute values and `start_bpm`/`end_bpm` — both were\\n  already known-bad from earlier tries (recorded in watch-notes.md) and the\\n  generator never emitted them.\\n- Surveying the Intervals UI target dropdown — wrong turn in the note; the\\n  dropdown picks the units label, not the value shape.\\n- The bonus `description` text-grammar trap (steps invisible in the Intervals\\n  web UI, `m` = minutes, `mtr`/`km`, bare `Z2` = power zones, etc.). That\\n  fixes web-UI rendering, which was not part of the reported symptom —\\n  watch-notes.md only documents the watch clamping. Left out to keep the\\n  change minimal; worth revisiting separately if the web UI rendering\\n  matters.\\n\\n## Verification\\n\\n`python3 gen.py plans/tempo-run.json` now emits `\\\"start\\\": <lo>, \\\"end\\\": <hi>`\\nin every step's `hr` object. Remaining step: re-push the event and confirm on\\nthe watch that corridors show 151–160 / 129–141 etc., which per the note's\\nverified end-to-end recipe should now match Intervals' LTHR-172 math.\\n\"}"}}]}
