{"role":"assistant","content":"Fixed. The bug was in `hr_target()` at gen.py:13.\n\n**The problem:** the generator emitted HR targets as `{\"units\": \"%lthr\", \"value\": lo, \"end\": hi}`. Per the colleague's note, the key name `value` is what routes the FIT export as a Lap-HR / zone-mapped target — so the watch clamps each corridor to its own auto-detected zone bands. That's exactly the readback pattern: every displayed range was a Forerunner 965 zone edge, never 151–160 or 129–141.\n\n**The fix:** one-word rename — `value` → `start`. `{\"units\": \"%lthr\", \"start\": lo, \"end\": hi}` makes Intervals export an Instant-HR corridor, so the watch shows the raw bpm computed from Intervals' stored LTHR (172), immune to the watch's own zones. Regenerated output verified: all four steps now emit `start`/`end`.\n\nFull write-up is in ANSWER.md, including which parts of the colleague's note applied (the `value`→`start` diagnosis, and keeping `%lthr` percentages) and which I deliberately left out (the `description` text-grammar web-UI trap — different symptom, noted there for later).\n\nOne remaining step is yours: re-push with `./push.sh event.json` and confirm on the watch — expected corridors are now 117–129 / 151–160 / 129–141 / 112–124 bpm."}
